<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ruhuna</h1><p class="st-meta">200 BCE – 1070 CE (approx.) · capital Mahagama</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern kingdom of ancient Sri Lanka, base of rebels and liberators, from which Dutugemunu and Vijayabahu I set out to reunite the island.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavamsa">Mahavamsa</a>, Ruhuna was founded in the 3rd century BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanaga">Mahanaga</a>, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/anuradhapura-kingdom">Anuradhapura Kingdom</a>, who settled in the south-east of the island and built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tissamaharama">Mahagama</a>, modern Tissamaharama. Its rulers formed a branch of the Sinhalese royal line, sometimes independent, sometimes subordinate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ruhuna's dry south-east depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tissa-wewa">Tissa Wewa</a>, while ports like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godavaya">Godavaya</a> linked it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean-trade">Indian Ocean trade</a>. Its kings patronised Buddhist monasteries, and the shrine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kataragama">Kataragama</a> made it a pilgrimage region. Rugged and distant from the capital, it served for centuries as a refuge for princes and resistance against invaders; King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavan-tissa">Kavantissa</a> ruled there, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutugemunu">Dutugemunu</a> marched north to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ellalan">Elara</a> around 161 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> occupied northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the 11th century, Ruhuna became the centre of Sinhalese resistance. Its prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayabahu-i">Vijayabahu I</a> drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> in 1070 and founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/polonnaruwa-kingdom">Polonnaruwa Kingdom</a>, which reabsorbed Ruhuna, though it rebelled again in the 12th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ruhuna.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ruhuna" width="320" height="452"><figcaption>Image: Dotewathsala · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_trisinhalaya-locator-map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>200 BCE – 1070 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mahagama</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahanaga, Kavantissa, Vijayabahu I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sinhala</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ruhuna, Rohana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=435&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ruhuna" aria-label="Kingdom of Ruhuna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6904 1902 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ruhuna lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7224 2158L7222 2122M7224 2158L7240 2137"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7222 2122h0M7240 2137h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7224 2158h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7224 2158h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mahagama. <a href="/atlas?year=435&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ruhuna">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
